.TH DB2DELTA 1 "2025-07-10" "db2delta @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
db2delta \- 🚧 zero-dependency DB2 schema migration tool
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B db2delta
.I subcommand
.RI [ options ]
.PP
.B db2delta
.B apply
.B \-d
.I host[:port]/db
.B \-u
.I user
.RI [ options ]
.I file|dir\ .\|.\|.
.PP
.B db2delta
.B exec
.B \-d
.I host[:port]/db
.B \-u
.I user
.RI [ options ]
.I SQL
.PP
.B db2delta
.B version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B db2delta
🚧 is a statically-compiled, zero-dependency DB2 schema migration tool.
No Java, no XML, no DB2 client libraries required.
It ships as a single static binary and speaks the DRDA protocol directly.
.PP
Delta files contain versioned SQL changesets.
.B db2delta
records applied changesets in a changelog table in the target database
and skips changesets that have already been applied, making it safe to
run migrations idempotently.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B apply
Connect to DB2 and apply one or more delta files or directories of delta files.
Skips changesets whose SHA-256 hash is already recorded in the changelog table.
.TP
.B exec
Connect to DB2, execute a single SQL statement, and print the results to
standard output.
.TP
.B version
Print the db2delta version string and exit.
.TP
.B help
Print a brief usage summary and exit.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-database " " \fIhost[:port]/db\fR
DB2 connection string.
.I host
is the hostname or IP address of the DB2 server.
.I port
defaults to
.B 50000
if omitted.
.I db
is the database name (e.g.\& \fBMYDB\fR).
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-user " " \fIuser\fR
DB2 username.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-password " " \fIpassword\fR
DB2 password.
If omitted and
.B \-\-script
is not set,
.B db2delta
prompts for the password on the terminal with echo disabled (the typed
characters are not displayed).
In script mode
.RB ( \-x )
a missing password is a fatal error.
If
.B DB2_PASSWORD
is set in
.I database.env
no prompt is issued.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-continue\-on\-error
Do not abort on a changeset error.
By default
.B db2delta
stops and rolls back the current changeset on the first error.
With this flag it logs the failure and continues with the next changeset.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-script
Script mode.
Suppresses informational output and uses strict exit codes suitable for
use in CI pipelines and shell scripts.
Exit 0 on success, non-zero on any failure.
.SH DELTA FILE FORMAT
A delta file is a plain text file with a
.B .delta
extension.
It may contain one or more SQL statements separated by semicolons
.RB ( ; ).
.PP
To include stored procedures or other DDL that itself contains semicolons,
set the terminator to
.B @
in the file header:
.PP
.nf
.RS
-- terminator=@
CREATE PROCEDURE greet(IN name VARCHAR(64))
BEGIN
  DECLARE msg VARCHAR(128);
  SET msg = 'Hello, ' || name || '!';
END@
.RE
.fi
.PP
Each delta file is identified by its SHA-256 hash.
Once applied, the hash is recorded in the changelog table
.RB ( DB2DELTA_CHANGELOG )
and the changeset will not be re-applied on subsequent runs.
.SH PASSWORD PROMPT
If no password is available from
.B \-p
or
.IR database.env ,
.B db2delta
opens
.I /dev/tty
and prompts:
.PP
.nf
.RS
Password:
.RE
.fi
.PP
Terminal echo is disabled for the duration of the input so the password
is not visible on screen.
This mirrors the behaviour of tools such as
.BR ssh (1)
and
.BR sudo (1).
.PP
In script mode
.RB ( \-x )
interactive prompting is suppressed; a missing password causes an
immediate error exit (code 2) so that pipelines fail cleanly.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
If a file named
.I database.env
exists in the current working directory its
.BR key = value
pairs are read as defaults before command-line flags are processed.
Command-line flags always take precedence.
Recognised keys:
.TP
.B DB2_HOST
Equivalent to the
.B \-d
host portion.
.TP
.B DB2_PORT
Port number (default 50000).
.TP
.B DB2_DATABASE
Database name.
.TP
.B DB2_USER
Equivalent to
.BR \-u .
.TP
.B DB2_PASSWORD
Equivalent to
.BR \-p .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
One or more changesets failed (or the SQL statement returned an error).
.TP
.B 2
Invalid arguments or connection error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print the installed version:
.PP
.nf
.RS
db2delta version
.RE
.fi
.PP
Apply all delta files in a directory:
.PP
.nf
.RS
db2delta apply \-d myhost/MYDB \-u db2inst1 migrations/
.RE
.fi
.PP
Apply a single delta file, continuing past errors:
.PP
.nf
.RS
db2delta apply \-d myhost/MYDB \-u db2inst1 \-c 001_add_users.delta
.RE
.fi
.PP
Execute an ad-hoc SQL statement:
.PP
.nf
.RS
db2delta exec \-d myhost/MYDB \-u db2inst1 \e
    "SELECT TABNAME FROM SYSCAT.TABLES WHERE TABSCHEMA='DB2INST1'"
.RE
.fi
.PP
Use a
.I database.env
file to avoid repeating connection flags:
.PP
.nf
.RS
echo "DB2_HOST=myhost
DB2_DATABASE=MYDB
DB2_USER=db2inst1
DB2_PASSWORD=secret" > database.env
db2delta apply migrations/
.RE
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ./database.env
Optional per-project connection defaults.
.TP
.I /etc/bash_completion.d/db2delta
Bash tab-completion script installed with the package.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR db2 (1)
